{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Deep Chlorophyll Maximum: Diatom-Bacteria Coupling at the Nutricline",
    "date": "2026-09-29",
    "lesson": "In the stratified subtropical gyre, surface waters are sunny but starved of nutrients, while deep water is nutrient-rich but dark. Between them sits the deep chlorophyll maximum (DCM): a thin band where just enough light and just enough upwelled nitrate/phosphate overlap to support a standing crop of diatoms and phototrophic bacteria. Heterotrophic bacteria cluster in the phycosphere around these cells, living on their exuded carbon. Drop below the DCM and food gets nutrient-rich but photosynthesis stops; rise above it and light is abundant but the diatoms starve. The player must find and hold that narrow band.",
    "citation": "Cullen, J.J. (1982) 'The deep chlorophyll maximum: comparing vertical profiles of chlorophyll a.' Can. J. Fish. Aquat. Sci.",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Deep chlorophyll maximum layers in stratified oligotrophic seas (subtropical gyres, Mediterranean)"
  },
